            {
              "id": "glog-2624b522-2d92-4d1b-a0ba-0c1ce79e4635",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\n\nThe \"Unsecured URL\" vulnerability in C++ programming occurs when a program constructs URLs without proper validation or sanitization, potentially leading to security issues such as information leakage, man-in-the-middle attacks, or redirection to malicious sites. This vulnerability can be exploited if user input is directly used to form URLs without adequate checks.\n\n### General Mitigation Advice\n\n1. **Input Validation**: Always validate and sanitize user inputs before using them to construct URLs.\n2. **Use Secure Protocols**: Prefer HTTPS over HTTP to ensure data is encrypted during transmission.\n3. **Whitelist URLs**: Implement a whitelist of allowed URLs to prevent redirection to unauthorized sites.\n4. **Library Functions**: Use library functions that handle URL construction and validation securely.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nBelow is a C++ code example demonstrating a potential fix for an unsecured URL vulnerability. The example uses the `cpp-netlib` library to safely construct and validate URLs.\n\n```cpp\n#include <iostream>\n#include <boost/network/protocol/http/client.hpp>\n\nint main() {\n    using namespace boost::network;\n    using namespace boost::network::http;\n\n    // User input\n    std::string user_input = \"wikipedia.org\";\n\n    // Validate and construct URL\n    if (user_input.find(\"http://\") == std::string::npos && user_input.find(\"https://\") == std::string::npos) {\n        user_input = \"https://\" + user_input;\n    }\n\n    try {\n        client::request request(user_input);\n        client client_;\n        client::response response = client_.get(request);\n\n        std::cout << body(response) << std::endl;\n    } catch (std::exception &e) {\n        std::cerr << \"Error: \" << e.what() << std::endl;\n    }\n\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\n### Library Dependencies\n\nTo execute the above code example, the following library dependencies are required:\n\n- Boost C++ Libraries (specifically `cpp-netlib`)\n\n### OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [OWASP Secure Coding Practices](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-coding-practices-quick-reference-guide/)\n\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-601: URL Redirection to Untrusted Site ('Open Redirect')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/601.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS137138"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-6d50ee7d-bac2-4e37-8a88-54d097bed590",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\n\nThe \"Unsecured URL\" vulnerability in C++ programming occurs when a program constructs URLs using untrusted input without proper validation or sanitization. This can lead to various security issues, such as URL redirection attacks, information disclosure, or even remote code execution if the URL is used to download and execute code. Attackers can manipulate the URL to redirect users to malicious sites or access sensitive data.\n\n### General Mitigation Advice\n\n1. **Input Validation**: Always validate and sanitize input used to construct URLs. Ensure that the input conforms to expected patterns and does not contain malicious content.\n2. **Use Secure Libraries**: Utilize libraries that handle URL construction and validation securely.\n3. **HTTPS Enforcement**: Always use HTTPS URLs to ensure secure communication.\n4. **Whitelist URLs**: Implement a whitelist of allowed URLs or domains to prevent redirection to unauthorized sites.\n5. **Error Handling**: Implement robust error handling to manage unexpected input gracefully.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nBelow is a code example demonstrating a potential fix for an unsecured URL vulnerability in C++:\n\n```cpp\n#include <iostream>\n#include <string>\n#include <regex>\n#include <stdexcept>\n\n// Function to validate URL\nbool isValidURL(const std::string& url) {\n    const std::regex urlPattern(\"(https?://)([\\\\w.-]+)(:[0-9]+)?(/.*)?\");\n    return std::regex_match(url, urlPattern);\n}\n\nvoid openURL(const std::string& url) {\n    if (!isValidURL(url)) {\n        throw std::invalid_argument(\"Invalid URL\");\n    }\n    // Proceed with opening the URL securely\n    std::cout << \"Opening URL: \" << url << std::endl;\n}\n\nint main() {\n    try {\n        std::string userInputURL = \"http://randomascii.wordpress.com\";\n        openURL(userInputURL);\n    } catch (const std::exception& e) {\n        std::cerr << \"Error: \" << e.what() << std::endl;\n    }\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\n### Library Dependencies\n\n- `<iostream>`: For input and output operations.\n- `<string>`: For string manipulation.\n- `<regex>`: For regular expression operations.\n- `<stdexcept>`: For standard exception handling.\n\n### OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [OWASP Cheat Sheet Series](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/)\n\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-601: URL Redirection to Untrusted Site ('Open Redirect')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/601.html)\n\nThese links were verified to be active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS137138"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-c3282993-c389-4cf6-837c-29d2ab4101c2",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\n\nThe \"Unsecured URL\" vulnerability in C++ programming occurs when a program constructs URLs without proper validation or sanitization, potentially leading to security issues such as information leakage, man-in-the-middle attacks, or redirection to malicious sites. This vulnerability can be exploited if user input is directly used to form URLs without adequate checks.\n\n### General Mitigation Advice\n\n1. **Input Validation**: Always validate and sanitize user inputs before using them to construct URLs.\n2. **Use Secure Protocols**: Prefer HTTPS over HTTP to ensure data is encrypted during transmission.\n3. **Whitelist URLs**: Implement a whitelist of allowed URLs to prevent redirection to unauthorized sites.\n4. **Library Functions**: Use library functions that handle URL construction and validation securely.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nBelow is a C++ code example demonstrating a potential fix for an unsecured URL vulnerability. The example uses the `cpp-netlib` library to safely construct and validate URLs.\n\n```cpp\n#include <iostream>\n#include <boost/network/protocol/http/client.hpp>\n\nint main() {\n    using namespace boost::network;\n    using namespace boost::network::http;\n\n    // User input\n    std::string user_input = \"wikipedia.org\";\n\n    // Validate and construct URL\n    if (user_input.find(\"http://\") == std::string::npos && user_input.find(\"https://\") == std::string::npos) {\n        user_input = \"https://\" + user_input;\n    }\n\n    try {\n        client::request request(user_input);\n        client client_;\n        client::response response = client_.get(request);\n\n        std::cout << body(response) << std::endl;\n    } catch (std::exception &e) {\n        std::cerr << \"Error: \" << e.what() << std::endl;\n    }\n\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\n### Library Dependencies\n\nTo execute the above code example, the following library dependencies are required:\n\n- Boost C++ Libraries (specifically `cpp-netlib`)\n\n### OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [OWASP Secure Coding Practices](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-coding-practices-quick-reference-guide/)\n\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-601: URL Redirection to Untrusted Site ('Open Redirect')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/601.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS137138"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-e3a44c87-828f-498e-ae7c-44beb169d707",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\n\nThe \"Unsecured URL\" vulnerability in C++ programming occurs when a program constructs URLs using untrusted input without proper validation or sanitization. This can lead to various security issues, such as URL redirection attacks, information disclosure, or even command injection if the URL is used in system calls. Attackers can manipulate the URL to redirect users to malicious sites or execute unintended actions.\n\n### General Mitigation Advice\n\n1. **Input Validation**: Always validate and sanitize input used to construct URLs. Ensure that the input conforms to expected patterns and does not contain malicious content.\n2. **Use Secure Libraries**: Utilize libraries that provide secure URL handling and construction functions.\n3. **Avoid Direct URL Construction**: Use high-level abstractions or APIs for URL construction instead of manually concatenating strings.\n4. **Implement Whitelisting**: Allow only known and trusted URLs or domains.\n5. **HTTPS Enforcement**: Ensure that URLs use HTTPS to prevent man-in-the-middle attacks.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nBelow is an example of how to securely handle URLs in C++:\n\n```cpp\n#include <iostream>\n#include <string>\n#include <regex>\n#include <stdexcept>\n\n// Function to validate and construct a secure URL\nstd::string constructSecureURL(const std::string& input) {\n    // Define a regex pattern for a simple URL validation\n    const std::regex urlPattern(R\"(^https:\\/\\/[a-zA-Z0-9.-]+\\.[a-zA-Z]{2,6}(\\/\\S*)?$)\");\n\n    // Validate the input URL\n    if (!std::regex_match(input, urlPattern)) {\n        throw std::invalid_argument(\"Invalid URL format\");\n    }\n\n    // Construct the URL\n    std::string secureURL = input;\n\n    // Additional security checks can be added here\n\n    return secureURL;\n}\n\nint main() {\n    try {\n        std::string userInput = \"https://www.parashift.com\";\n        std::string secureURL = constructSecureURL(userInput);\n        std::cout << \"Secure URL: \" << secureURL << std::endl;\n    } catch (const std::exception& e) {\n        std::cerr << \"Error: \" << e.what() << std::endl;\n    }\n\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\n### Library Dependencies\n\n- `<iostream>`: For input and output operations.\n- `<string>`: For string manipulation.\n- `<regex>`: For regular expression operations.\n- `<stdexcept>`: For standard exception handling.\n\n### OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [OWASP Cheat Sheet Series](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/)\n\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-601: URL Redirection to Untrusted Site ('Open Redirect')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/601.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS137138"
                ]
              }
