              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `memcpy` function in C++ is used to copy a block of memory from one location to another. This function does not check for any terminating null character in source - it always copies exactly num bytes. This can lead to buffer overflow if the destination buffer size is less than the source buffer size. In the provided code snippet, `memcpy` is copying the content of `isa_name` into `value` without checking if `value` has enough space to hold the content of `isa_name`.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the data being copied. This can be done by checking the size of the destination buffer and the size of the source buffer before the `memcpy` operation. If the source buffer is larger than the destination buffer, do not perform the `memcpy` operation.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code snippet:\n\n```cpp\nif (isa_name.size() < sizeof(value)) {\n    memcpy(value, isa_name.c_str(), isa_name.size());\n} else {\n    // Handle error: destination buffer is too small\n}\n```\n\nIn this fixed code, `memcpy` is only called if `value` is large enough to hold the content of `isa_name`. If `value` is not large enough, an error is handled instead.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstring>`: This library provides the `memcpy` function.\n- `<string>`: This library provides the `std::string` class used for `isa_name`.\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are valid and active at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-e7dd629f-93e1-47af-bb31-467a5a8e1bf3",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc` function in C++ is used to dynamically allocate memory at runtime. However, it can be a source of vulnerabilities if not used properly. If the `config_cu_param_size` variable is controlled by the user or can be manipulated in any way, it can lead to a buffer overflow vulnerability. This is because `malloc` does not initialize the memory it allocates, thus potentially leading to undefined behavior if the memory is accessed before being initialized.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always validate and sanitize user input. Never trust data provided by the user. Also, consider using functions that limit the amount of data that can be written to the memory, such as `calloc`, which not only allocates memory but also initializes it to zero.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `malloc` function with the `calloc` function. The `calloc` function takes two arguments: the number of elements and the size of each element. It also initializes the allocated memory to zero.\n\n```cpp\n// Old vulnerable code\n// void* data = malloc(config_cu_param_size);\n\n// New fixed code\nvoid* data = calloc(1, config_cu_param_size);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc` and `calloc` functions are part of the C standard library, so no additional libraries are needed.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-9c5f924c-b3b7-47c9-be55-bef84f216f21",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is a buffer overflow vulnerability that can occur in the `memcpy` function in C++. This function is used to copy a block of memory from one location to another. If the size of the destination buffer is less than the size of the source buffer, it can lead to a buffer overflow. This can overwrite adjacent memory and can lead to unexpected behavior, including program crashes, incorrect results, or a security vulnerability.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the vulnerability arises if `4 * pkt->count` is greater than the size of the destination buffer `(cmd->data + 1)`. This can lead to a buffer overflow.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the data being copied. This can be done by checking the size of the destination buffer before calling `memcpy`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nA possible fix for the provided code snippet would be to add a check to ensure that `4 * pkt->count` does not exceed the size of the destination buffer. Here is an example:\n\n```cpp\nsize_t dest_size = sizeof(cmd->data) - 1; // -1 because we start from cmd->data + 1\nsize_t src_size = 4 * pkt->count;\n\nif (src_size <= dest_size) {\n    memcpy((cmd->data + 1), cmd_pkt_payload->data, src_size);\n} else {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `memcpy` function is part of the C Standard Library, so the code snippet requires the `<cstring>` header file in C++.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
