            {
              "id": "glog-be1110f0-7bc9-4d19-be6f-246699fd5622",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `memcpy` function in C++ is used to copy a block of memory from one location to another. This function is vulnerable to buffer overflow attacks if the size of the data being copied is larger than the size of the destination buffer. In the provided code snippet, `memcpy(log_flags, flags, sizeof(log_flags))`, if the size of `flags` is larger than `log_flags`, it can lead to a buffer overflow.\n\nBuffer overflow vulnerabilities can lead to various security issues such as data corruption, system crashes, and unauthorized code execution.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always ensure that the size of the source buffer is less than or equal to the size of the destination buffer. This can be done by checking the size of the source buffer before copying it to the destination buffer.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code snippet:\n\n```cpp\nsize_t size = sizeof(log_flags);\nif (sizeof(flags) <= size) {\n    memcpy(log_flags, flags, size);\n} else {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\nIn this fix, we first check if the size of `flags` is less than or equal to the size of `log_flags`. If it is, we proceed with the `memcpy`. If it's not, we handle the error appropriately.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `memcpy` function is part of the C Standard Library, so no additional library dependencies are required for the provided code snippet.\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are valid and active for anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
