            {
              "id": "glog-e12fd0be-abe0-4d2e-abbf-73879c81d55e",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strncpy` function in C++ is a common source of vulnerabilities due to its potential to cause buffer overflows. This function copies a specified number of characters from one string to another. If the source string is longer than the specified number of characters, `strncpy` does not append a null character to the destination string. This can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities if the destination string is used in a context that expects it to be null-terminated.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, consider using safer string handling functions that ensure null-termination and prevent buffer overflows. For example, you can use `strlcpy` if it's available on your system. Alternatively, you can manually ensure null-termination by setting the last character of the destination string to null after calling `strncpy`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere's how you can fix the vulnerability in the provided code:\n\n```cpp\nstrncpy(address.sun_path, socketName, IPC_SOCK_SERVER_NAME_LENGTH - 1);\naddress.sun_path[IPC_SOCK_SERVER_NAME_LENGTH - 1] = '\\0';\n```\n\nIn this code, we copy one less character than the buffer size to leave space for the null character, which we then manually append.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `#include <string.h>` for `strncpy`\n- `#include <sys/un.h>` for `sockaddr_un`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\nPlease note that the availability and behavior of `strncpy`, `strlcpy`, and other string handling functions can vary between different systems and C++ standard libraries. Always refer to your system's and compiler's documentation for the most accurate information."
