              "id": "glog-88c08086-8d5c-49b2-a6c6-dacf1b3e9a4d",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the `malloc()` function in C++. `malloc()` is a function that allocates a certain amount of memory during the execution of a program. It will request a block of memory from the heap. If the request is granted, the operating system will reserve the requested amount of memory.\n\nThe vulnerability arises when the argument passed to `malloc()` is a value that can be manipulated by an attacker, leading to a buffer overflow. In this case, the argument is `getpagesize()`, which returns the number of bytes in a page. If an attacker can manipulate this value, they can cause the program to allocate too much or too little memory, leading to a buffer overflow or underflow.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should avoid using functions that can cause buffer overflows, such as `malloc()`. Instead, use functions that limit the amount of memory that can be allocated, such as `calloc()`. Additionally, always validate and sanitize inputs to prevent an attacker from manipulating them.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `malloc(getpagesize())` function with `calloc(1, getpagesize())`. This will allocate memory for an array of 1 element, each of which is `getpagesize()` bytes long. This prevents the possibility of a buffer overflow.\n\n```cpp\n// Old vulnerable code\nchar *buffer = (char *) malloc(getpagesize());\n\n// Recommended fix\nchar *buffer = (char *) calloc(1, getpagesize());\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `stdlib.h` for the `malloc()` and `calloc()` functions.\n- `unistd.h` for the `getpagesize()` function.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
