            {
              "id": "glog-01c9f737-48a7-414a-8047-5e8cb82505fd",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc` function in C++ is used to dynamically allocate memory at runtime. The vulnerability arises when the `malloc` function is used improperly, leading to memory leaks, buffer overflows, or other memory-related issues. In the provided code snippet, `malloc(4096)`, 4096 bytes of memory are being allocated, but there is no check to ensure that the allocation was successful. If the system is unable to allocate the requested memory, `malloc` will return a null pointer, which can lead to undefined behavior if not properly handled.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always check the return value of `malloc` to ensure that the memory allocation was successful. If `malloc` returns a null pointer, take appropriate action to handle the error condition.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix for the provided code snippet:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\nvoid* ptr = malloc(4096);\nif (ptr == NULL) {\n    // Handle error condition here, e.g., by exiting the program or by attempting to free up memory and trying again\n    exit(EXIT_FAILURE);\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc` function is part of the C standard library, so the only library dependency required by the code example is `stdlib.h`.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-690: Unchecked Return Value to NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/690.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
