            {
              "id": "glog-136a8162-57ff-4b3c-9448-62274b6ca953",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the `malloc` function in C++. This function is used to allocate a block of memory of a specified size. The size is specified in bytes and the function returns a pointer to the first byte of the allocated block. If the function fails to allocate the requested block of memory, it returns a null pointer.\n\nThe vulnerability arises when the size of the memory to be allocated is controlled by user input. This can lead to several types of vulnerabilities including buffer overflows, integer overflows, and denial of service attacks. If an attacker can control the size of the memory to be allocated, they can cause the program to allocate too much memory, leading to a crash or other unexpected behavior.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always validate user input before using it in a function like `malloc`. You should also consider using functions that limit the amount of memory that can be allocated, such as `calloc`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix for the vulnerability. Instead of directly passing the user input to `malloc`, we first check if the input is within an acceptable range.\n\n```cpp\nif (input->sizeInBytes > 0 && input->sizeInBytes <= MAX_SIZE) {\n    void* ptr = malloc(input->sizeInBytes);\n    if (ptr == NULL) {\n        // handle error\n    }\n    // use ptr\n} else {\n    // handle error\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, `MAX_SIZE` is a constant that defines the maximum amount of memory that can be allocated. You should set this constant to a value that is appropriate for your application.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc` function is part of the C standard library, so no additional libraries are needed for this code to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-789: Uncontrolled Memory Allocation](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/789.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
