            {
              "id": "glog-6cfa1a57-f7c5-4012-a850-7eec1c306302",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc` function in C is used to dynamically allocate memory at runtime. However, it can lead to vulnerabilities if not used properly. If the size of the memory to be allocated is not properly validated or if the return value of `malloc` (which is a pointer to the allocated memory) is not checked for `NULL`, it can lead to vulnerabilities such as buffer overflow, null pointer dereference, or memory leaks.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always validate the size of the memory to be allocated and check the return value of `malloc` for `NULL`. If `malloc` returns `NULL`, it means that the memory allocation failed, and any attempt to access this memory will result in undefined behavior.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix for the code:\n\n```c++\npthread_t* thread = (pthread_t*) malloc(sizeof(pthread_t));\nif (thread == NULL) {\n    // handle error\n} else {\n    // use thread\n    free(thread);\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, we first allocate memory for a `pthread_t` object using `malloc`. Then, we check if the return value is `NULL`. If it is, we handle the error (for example, by logging an error message and exiting the program). If it's not `NULL`, we use the `pthread_t` object and then free the memory when we're done.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `pthread.h`: This is the POSIX thread library, which provides the `pthread_t` type and related functions.\n- `stdlib.h`: This is the standard library, which provides the `malloc` and `free` functions.\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP C/C++ Vulnerability Guide](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_Quick_Reference_Guide_v2.pdf)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)\n- [CWE-690: Unchecked Return Value to NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/690.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are valid and active for anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-9458633f-48c8-4fde-bae3-7aca5bb1ba07",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc` function in C is used to dynamically allocate memory at runtime. However, it can be a source of vulnerabilities if not used correctly. If the size of the memory to be allocated is not properly validated or controlled, it can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, memory corruption, or denial of service attacks.\n\nIn the given code snippet, `malloc(sizeof(thread_aux))`, the vulnerability could arise if the size of `thread_aux` is not properly controlled or if the return value of `malloc` is not checked for `NULL`. If `malloc` fails to allocate the requested memory, it returns `NULL`, and any attempt to use this `NULL` pointer would result in undefined behavior, potentially leading to a program crash or other vulnerabilities.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\n1. Always check the return value of `malloc` for `NULL` before using the allocated memory.\n2. Be careful with the size of the memory you are trying to allocate. Make sure it is not too large to cause integer overflows or too small to cause buffer overflows.\n3. Consider using functions that limit the amount of memory that can be written, such as `calloc` or `strncpy`, instead of `malloc` and `strcpy`.\n4. Use a modern, high-level language that manages memory for you whenever possible.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix for the given code snippet:\n\n```c\nthread_aux *aux = (thread_aux *) malloc(sizeof(thread_aux));\nif (aux == NULL) {\n    // Handle error\n    fprintf(stderr, \"Failed to allocate memory.\\n\");\n    exit(1);\n}\n```\n\nIn this fixed code, we check if `malloc` returns `NULL` and handle the error appropriately.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc` function is part of the standard C library (`stdlib.h`), so no additional libraries are needed for this code snippet.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
